<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-1-further-integration-techniques" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Further Integration Techniques</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> Up to this point, we have learned how to integrate all of the most common functions, but
        our toolkit is not robust enough to handle every function we might encounter. In this chapter, we will
        begin by studying integration by parts, which will allow us to integrate products of known functions.
        We will then learn how to integrate complicated trigonometric expressions, and how we can use this insight
        to integrate functions that do not look trigonometric in nature. We will also learn how decomposing fractions
        into simpler pieces can help us integrate rational functions. Finally, we will also learn how to deal with integrals
        involving singularities or infinite limits of integration.
    </p>
  </introduction>

